<article class="env-lemma" id="0BD7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BD7">Lemma <span data-tag="0BD7">6.5.1.9</span>.</a> Let $X$ be an object in an $\infty $-topos $\calX $, and let $p: X \rightarrow Y$ be an $n$-truncation of $X$. Then $p$ induces isomorphisms $\pi _{k}(X) \simeq p^{\ast } \pi _{k}(Y)$ for all $k \leq n$. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\phi : \calX \rightarrow \calY $ be a geometric morphism such that $\phi _{\ast }$ is fully faithful. By Proposition <a href="/tag/E855" data-tag="E855">5.5.6.28</a> and Remark <a href="/tag/BEDF" data-tag="BEDF">6.5.1.4</a>, it will suffice to prove the lemma in the case where $\calX = \calY $. We may therefore assume that $\calY $ is an $\infty $-category of presheaves. In this case, homotopy groups and truncations are computed pointwise. Thus we may reduce to the case $\calX = \SSet $, where the conclusion follows from classical homotopy theory. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>